  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal agencies obligated $5.6B for Security Guards and Patrol Services (NAICS 561612) in FY2026 to date. There were 4,951 awards with an average award size of $1.1M, and obligations fell 6.5% year over year. Obligations have ranged from $5B in FY21 to $6B in FY25, and the FY2026 to date total matches FY23 and FY24 at $5.6B. The Department of Homeland Security dominates purchasing with $3.2B in this period. Other sizable buyers include the Department of State at $789M and the Department of Justice at $758M. The market is concentrated among a handful of large vendors: METROPOLITAN SECURITY SERVICES, INC. received $530M, and AMENTUM SERVICES, INC. received $504M. THE GEO GROUP, INC. and two other firms each attracted hundreds of millions in obligations. This is a services-heavy market characterized by many mid-sized awards alongside a few very large agency programs and prime contractors that hold a substantial share of obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 6.5% year over year. The FY2026 to date total is $5.6B, the same as FY23 and FY24 and below FY25 at $6B. Award volume remains substantial at 4,951 awards, with an average award size of $1.1M, which shows continued fragmentation across many contract actions."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominate the market?",
        "body": "The Department of Homeland Security leads buyers with $3.2B in obligations, followed by the Department of State at $789M and the Department of Justice at $758M. On the vendor side, METROPOLITAN SECURITY SERVICES, INC. tops the list with $530M, followed by AMENTUM SERVICES, INC. at $504M and THE GEO GROUP, INC. at $442M, indicating a concentrated prime contractor set."
      }
    ]
  },
